You have noticed something off about one of your fingernails. Maybe it has gone slightly yellow. Maybe it is thicker than it used to be, or the edge has started to crumble. You have read that <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> is relatively uncommon, which makes you second-guess what you are seeing. And most of the content you find is about toenails, not hands, leaving your actual question unanswered.

This post is specifically about <strong>fingernail fungus</strong>: how it differs from what happens on toenails, what causes it, what it looks like, and what recovery realistically involves. If your concern is a hand nail, the toenail information most sources lead with is not especially useful. The differences are real, and they matter for how you respond to what you are seeing.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## What Fingernail Fungus Actually Is

<strong>Fingernail fungus</strong> is a fungal infection of the nail plate, nail bed, or both, medically known as onychomycosis. The umbrella condition covers all nail fungal infections regardless of location, and the broader <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/what-is-nail-fungus'>types of nail fungus</a> include several distinct clinical patterns that behave differently depending on where they appear.

Distal subungual onychomycosis is the most common fungal nail infection type, occurring in both fingernails and toenails but far more frequently on toes. It begins at the free edge of the nail and progresses inward toward the nail matrix. On a fingernail, it presents as a whitish or yellowish discolouration under the tip, often with mild thickening.

Candidal onychomycosis is a yeast-driven fungal nail infection more likely to affect fingernails than toenails. Rather than entering from the nail tip, it typically begins at the nail folds, the skin surrounding the nail, and is associated with swelling and redness around the nail itself. This is a meaningful distinction from toenail infections, where yeast involvement is less typical.

What both types share: they do not resolve on their own. Without antifungal treatment, the infection progresses, and the nail continues to deteriorate.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Why Fingernail Fungus Is Less Common Than Nail Fungus on the Toes

<strong>Fingernail fungus</strong> is less common than its toenail equivalent because of two compounding environmental factors: air exposure and nail growth rate. Fingernails spend most of the day in open air, drying out between uses. Toenails live inside shoes, in warm and damp conditions that favour fungal growth. That difference in microenvironment is the core of why toenail infections are far more prevalent.

Fingernails also grow roughly four times faster than toenails. The average fingernail grows about 3 millimetres per month; toenails grow closer to 1 millimetre. Faster growth means the nail replaces infected tissue more quickly, which makes fingernails somewhat more resilient to establishing deep infections and significantly faster to clear once treatment begins.

A third factor involves fungal type. The dermatophytes responsible for most cases of onychomycosis thrive in the specific environment of shoes and floors. They reach toenails through direct contact with contaminated surfaces. Fingernails are simply less likely to encounter those routes of transmission. While dermatophytes can and do infect fingernails, the route is different, usually hand-to-foot or skin-to-nail contact rather than barefoot exposure. Candida species, which do not depend on the same warm-shoe environment, account for a proportionally higher share of fingernail infections precisely because they reach the nail via a different pathway: chronically wet skin.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## How Fingernail Fungus Looks Different in Early and Later Stages

Most people picture nail fungus as thick, yellowed, and obviously abnormal. That is advanced onychomycosis. Early <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> looks like almost nothing at all, which is one reason people second-guess whether what they are seeing is worth addressing.

In the early stages, the most common signs are a small white or yellowish spot under the tip of the nail or near the base, slight lifting of the nail from the nail bed, or a mild dullness where the nail used to be shiny. The change is subtle enough to be mistaken for a bruise, a water stain, or nothing significant.

As the infection progresses, the nail plate itself begins to thicken. The discolouration spreads inward and deepens, moving from white or pale yellow toward tan, brown, or darker yellow. The texture changes. Crumbling at the edge is a sign the nail structure itself is being degraded. In more advanced cases, the nail lifts from the bed partially or fully, a process called onycholysis, which allows more fungal material to accumulate underneath.

Where candidal <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> looks distinctly different from the dermatophyte pattern is at the nail folds. Redness, swelling, and tenderness around the sides and base of the nail are hallmarks of candidal infection. This combination of nail change and surrounding skin inflammation is called paronychia, and it tends to be visually obvious even in relatively early stages.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## What Causes Fungus to Get Under a Fingernail

Fungi enter the fingernail through small breaks in the skin surrounding the nail or through the gap between the nail plate and nail bed. These entry points are microscopic, which is why nail fungus can establish without any obvious injury or event that the person can point to.

Candidal onychomycosis is a yeast-driven fungal nail infection more likely to affect fingernails than toenails, driven largely by moisture. Candida species colonise skin that is frequently wet, and when water exposure creates small cracks or softens the nail folds, the yeast finds entry. This is why <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> caused by candida is specifically associated with occupations and habits involving wet hands.

The dermatophyte pathway to fingernails is usually skin-mediated. A dermatophyte infection somewhere on the body, typically athlete's foot on the feet or tinea on the hands, serves as the reservoir. From there, the fungus spreads to the nails through repeated direct contact. Scratching an infected foot and then touching a fingernail, for example, is a plausible transmission route, even if it sounds unlikely.

Nail trauma is also a meaningful factor. A break in the nail plate, whether from a heavy object, repeated pressure, or overly aggressive cuticle work, creates a physical entry point that a healthy nail would not have. Salon tools that are not properly sterilised between clients are a well-documented transmission route, particularly for <strong>fingernail fungus</strong>, since nail technicians work directly on the nail plate and surrounding skin. According to the <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/a-z/nail-fungus-causes">American Academy of Dermatology</a>, age, poor circulation, and immune status all contribute to susceptibility alongside these environmental factors.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Who Is Most at Risk of Fingernail Fungus

People who frequently work with their hands in wet environments face the highest occupational risk. Dishwashers, kitchen staff, cleaners, carers, healthcare workers, and nail technicians all spend significant time with wet or damp hands, and that sustained moisture exposure is the primary risk pathway for candidal <strong>fingernail fungus</strong>.

Two feet-one hand syndrome is a recognised pattern where a fungal infection present on both feet spreads to one hand, most often the dominant hand used to touch or scratch the feet. People who already have athlete's foot or existing nail fungus on their toes are at heightened risk of fingernail involvement through this pathway, even without an obvious moment of cross-infection.

Other risk factors are shared with onychomycosis more broadly: older age (nails grow more slowly and repair more slowly), diabetes (which affects circulation and immune response), psoriasis (which alters nail structure), and a suppressed immune system. According to the <a href="https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/21712-mycotic-nails">Cleveland Clinic's overview of candidal onychomycosis</a>, conditions that compromise immune function specifically increase susceptibility to yeast-driven infections, which disproportionately affect fingernails.

Frequent manicures are a real risk factor when salon hygiene standards are inconsistent. Shared files, buffers, and clippers that are not sterilised between clients can transfer both dermatophyte and yeast fungi. Gel nail application and removal, which involves breaking the seal of the natural nail and prolonged acetone exposure to the surrounding skin, creates conditions that increase vulnerability, particularly when repeated frequently.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## How Fingernail Fungus Treatment and Recovery Time Compares to Toenails

With treatment, <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> typically clears in 3 to 6 months. Toenail infections require 12 to 18 months for full visible clearance. The difference is almost entirely explained by nail growth rate. Treatment kills the fungus; the nail still has to grow out to replace the infected plate. A faster-growing fingernail simply does this work more quickly.

For context, terbinafine, the most commonly prescribed oral antifungal, is taken daily for 6 weeks for fingernail infections and for 12 weeks for toenail infections. Those different durations reflect the same growth-rate logic: the drug course is calibrated to the time needed for healthy nail to advance and replace the infected portion.

Topical antifungal treatments, such as efinaconazole solution or amorolfine nail lacquer, are an option for mild infections or when systemic treatment is not appropriate. Their limitation is penetration: the nail plate is a significant barrier, and topical agents are better suited to superficial or early infections than to established deep ones. For moderate to severe <strong>fingernail fungus</strong>, or infections affecting more than one nail, oral therapy consistently shows higher cure rates. Full details on the options are covered in the <a href='/blog/nail-fungus/nail-fungus-treatment'>treatment options for nail fungus</a>.

The practical point worth keeping in mind: even once treatment is working, the nail will not look immediately normal. The infected nail has to grow out and be replaced by healthy nail. The absence of new infected growth is the sign that treatment is succeeding, even when the existing nail still looks abnormal. For <a href="https://www.aad.org/public/diseases/a-z/nail-fungus-treatment">professional nail fungus treatment</a> options including nail avulsion for severe cases, a dermatologist can advise on whether mechanical removal of the nail plate would accelerate recovery.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## When Fingernail Changes Are Not Fungus at All

This section matters more for fingernails than for toenails. The range of conditions that can alter the appearance of a fingernail is broader, and <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> is statistically less likely to be the culprit than it would be on a toe. Before beginning any antifungal treatment, knowing what else might be happening is worth a moment.

Nail psoriasis affects approximately half of all people with psoriasis at some point. It can cause pitting (small depressions in the nail surface), onycholysis, discolouration, and nail bed thickening, all of which closely resemble fungal infection. The key clinical difference is that psoriasis tends to affect multiple nails simultaneously, often in a symmetrical pattern, and is frequently accompanied by other skin changes. <strong>Fingernail fungus</strong> more commonly begins on one nail.

Nail trauma from repeated impact, pressure, or mechanical injury causes discolouration and thickening that can appear almost identical to early onychomycosis. Trauma-related changes tend to involve the nail plate only, without soft tissue involvement, and typically have a traceable cause. A nail that changed after wearing tight shoes for a period, after a door slam, or after repeated sports impact is more likely trauma than infection.

Bacterial infection, specifically *Pseudomonas* contamination, can cause green or black discolouration under a nail lifted from the bed. This is not fungal and will not respond to antifungal treatment. It is a relatively common finding when water becomes trapped under a nail, and the colour is usually distinctive enough to differentiate it from the typical yellowing of <strong>fingernail fungus</strong>.

Dermatologists routinely recommend nail clippings or scrapings for culture before beginning systemic antifungal treatment, precisely because the visual overlap between these conditions is significant. Treating psoriasis with an antifungal achieves nothing; treating a fungal infection with a topical steroid appropriate for psoriasis can worsen it.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## When to See a Doctor About Fingernail Fungus

See a doctor when the nail change is spreading, when more than one nail is involved, or when you cannot confidently rule out another cause. Those are the clearest signals that self-assessment has reached its limit.

A dermatologist or GP can confirm whether <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> is the actual diagnosis. Clinical assessment alone is reasonably accurate, and for mild infections, a doctor may proceed to prescribe based on appearance. For more extensive or stubborn infections, nail clippings sent for culture identify the specific organism, which matters because candidal infections and dermatophyte infections sometimes respond to different treatment protocols.

The urgency is higher in specific circumstances: if you have diabetes, compromised circulation, or a suppressed immune system, even a mild-appearing nail infection warrants prompt professional assessment. In people with reduced immune function, infections that appear localised can progress more aggressively, and the threshold for systemic treatment is lower. If the skin around the nail is red, swollen, or painful, that adds a soft-tissue component that needs medical attention regardless of whether the underlying nail infection itself is severe.

A single discoloured fingernail with no other symptoms, in an otherwise healthy person, is not an emergency. But waiting too long extends both the infection and the treatment timeline. Acting at the first sign of spread is consistently the faster path to a clear nail.

<hr className="my-8 border-gray-200" />

## Your Fingernail Deserves a Specific Answer

The reason most <strong>fingernail fungus</strong> content is unsatisfying is that it is written with toenails as the default and hands as an afterthought. The conditions are related but not identical. Growth rate, fungal type, occupational risk, and treatment duration all differ in ways that actually matter for what you decide to do next.

What is worth taking from this: if your hand nail has changed in colour, texture, or thickness, you have good reason to investigate rather than wait. Fingernail fungus clears faster than its toenail equivalent, but only with treatment. And before you treat, confirming the diagnosis means you are not spending weeks on an antifungal for a nail that has psoriasis or a bruise underneath it.

The faster-growing nature of fingernails is, for once, an advantage. The recovery window is shorter, and the treatment courses are proportionally briefer. That is not a reason to delay acting, but it is a reason not to panic.
